## Run notes

- **blink resolvability** - Round 1's median sampling interval is ~334 ms, which is the mean spontaneous blink duration (334 ms, VanderWerf et al. 2003, J Neurophysiol 89(5):2784-96, doi:10.1152/jn.00557.2002 - spontaneous blink 334 +/- 67 ms). A blink therefore occupies 0-1 samples and blink RATE is not recoverable in Round 1 at any threshold; only extended closures are. Blink rate is reported for Round 2 (120 Hz) only.
- **viewing order** - 3/3 episodes whose heading states an order are reproduced by the recording timestamps; order for all 36 episodes is therefore taken from timestamps.
- **keystone composite** - engaged composite = mean within-subject z of pupil dilation, cognitive load, eye openness; gaze-deviation variance excluded because it is rate-dependent and the composite spans both rounds
- **keystone composite** - engaged composite = mean within-subject z of pupil dilation, cognitive load, eye openness; gaze-deviation variance excluded because it is rate-dependent and the composite spans both rounds
- **keystone composite** - engaged composite = mean within-subject z of pupil dilation, cognitive load, eye openness; gaze-deviation variance excluded because it is rate-dependent and the composite spans both rounds
- **EEG** - Every cell is reported regardless of retention. No cell is excluded for being noisy, because the retention and SNR figures ARE the evidence for the ruling that EEG was dropped as too noisy; removing the worst cells would remove the justification.

